  "raw_text": "315\n\nVERB TO NOUN #1 /\n \nDE\n \nVERBO\n \nA\n \nSUSTANTIVO #1\n \n–\n\nAGENT FORM / FORMA AGENTE\n\nFrom a verb can come a agent form noun - a person who (or a thing that) does the action of the verb. /\n\nDe un verbo puede venir un sustantivo en forma de agente: una persona que (o una cosa que) realiza la acción del\n\nverbo.\n\nThis is how to do it / Asi es como se hace:\n\n~\n \nIf the first letter of the verb is a consonant / Si la primera letra del verbo es una consonante:\n\nFirst letter of the verb (first 2 letters if they are both consonants) +\n \n“\na\n”\n \n+ verb root +\n \n“\nra\n”\n\nPrimera letra del verbo\n \n(primeras 2 letras si ambas son consonantes) + “\na\n” +\n \nraíz del verbo\n \n+ “\nra\n”\n\n~\n \nIf the first\n \nletter of the verb is a\n \nvowel\n \n/ Si la primera letra del verbo es una vocal:\n\n“\nÂ\ni\n \n” +\n \nverb\n \nroot\n \n+\n \n“\nra\n”\n\n“\nÂ\ni\n \n” +\n \nraíz del verbo\n \n+ “\nra\n”\n\nExamples where the verb starts with\n \na\n \nconsonant\n \n/\n \nEjemplos donde el verbo comienza con una consonante\n:\n\nBaikaia\n \n–\n \nto break\n \n/ romper\n \nB\n \n+\n \na\n \n+\n \nbaik\n \n+\n \nra\n \n→\n \nBabaikra\n \n–\n \none who breaks\n \n/ uno\n\nque se rompe\n\nBlikaia\n \n–\n \nto follow\n \n/ seguir\n \nBl\n \n+\n \na\n \n+\n \nblik\n \n+\n \nra\n \n→\n \nBlablikra\n \n–\n \none who follows,\n\nfollower\n \n/ uno que sigue, seguidor\n\nDaukaia\n \n–\n \nto do\n \n/ hacer\n \nD\n \n+\n \na\n \n+\n \ndauk\n \n+\n \nra\n \n→\n \nDadaukra\n \n–\n \ndoer\n \n/ hacedor\n\nInsla mangkaia\n \n–\n \nto plant a\n\ncrop\n \n/ plantar un cultivo\n\nM\n \n+\n \na\n \n+\n \nmangk\n \n+\n \nra\n \n→\n \nInsla mamangkra\n \n–\n \nsomeone who\n\nplants a crop,\n \nfarmer\n \n/ alguien q\nue\n\nsiembra cultivos, cultivador\n\nLâ krikaia\n \n–\n \nto break a law\n\n/ violar una ley\n\nKr\n \n+\n \na\n \n+\n \nkrik\n \n+\n \nra\n \n→\n \nL\nâ\n \nkrakrikra\n \n–\n \nlaw breaker\n \n/\n\ninfractor de la le\ny\n\nLihkaia\n \n–\n \nto like\n \n/ gustar\n \nL\n \n+\n \na\n \n+\n \nlihk\n \n+\n \nra\n \n→\n \nLalihkra\n \n–\n \nsomeone who likes\n \n/\n\nalguien a quien\n \nle guste\n\nMiskaia\n \n–\n \nto fish\n \n/ pescar\n \nM\n \n+\n \na\n \n+\n \nmisk\n \n+\n \nra\n \n→\n \nMamiskra\n \n–\n \nfisherman\n \n/ pescador\n\nMunaia\n \n–\n \nto do\n \n/ hacer\n \nM\n \n+\n \na\n \n+\n \nmun\n \n+\n \nra\n \n→\n \nMamunra\n \n–\n \ndoer\n \n/ hacedor\n\nRau munaia\n \n–\n \nto hurt\n\nsomeone\n \n/ lastimar a alguien\n\nM\n \n+\n \na\n \n+\n \nmun\n \n+\n \nra\n \n→\n \nRau mamunra\n \n–\n \nperson who hurts\n\no\nthers\n \n/ persona que lastima a\n\no\ntros\n\nPaskaia\n \n–\n \nto create\n \n/ crear\n \nP\n \n+\n \na\n \n+\n \npask\n \n+\n \nra\n \n→\n \nPapaskra\n \n–\n \ncreator\n \n/ creador\n\nPulaia\n \n–\n \nto play\n \n/ jugar\n \nP\n \n+\n \na\n \n+\n \npul\n \n+\n \nra\n \n→\n \nPapulra\n \n–\n \nplayer\n \n/ jugador\n\nSmalkaia\n \n–\n \nto teach\n \n/ enseñar\n \nSm\n \n+\n \na\n \n+\n \nsmalk\n \n+\n \nra\n \n→\n \nSmasmalkra\n \n–\n \nteacher\n/ profesor\n\nPura sunaia\n \n–\n \nto pray\n \n/ orar\n \nS\n \n+\n \na\n \n+\n \nsun\n \n+\n \nra\n \n→\n \nPura sasunra\n \n–\n \nperson who prays\n \n/\n\nalguien que ora\n\nTaibaia\n \n–\n \nto oppress, to cheat\n\n/ oprimir, engañar\n\nT\n \n+\n \na\n \n+\n \ntaib\n \n+\n \nra\n \n→\n \nTataibra\n \n–\n \noppressor, cheater\n \n/\n\nopresor, engaña\n\nTuskaia\n \n–\n \nto wash\n \n/ lavar\n \nT\n \n+\n \na\n \n+\n \ntusk\n \n+\n \nra\n \n→\n \nTatuskra\n \n–\n \ns\nomeone who washes\n \n/\n\nalguien que\n \nl\nava\n\nKwala tuskaia\n \n–\n \nto wash\n\nc\nlothes\n \n/ lavar la ropa\n\nT\n \n+\n \na\n \n+\n \ntusk\n \n+\n \nra\n \n→\n \nKwala tatuskra\n \n–\n \nsomeone who\n\nwashes clothes\n \n/ alguien que la\nva\n\nropa\n\nWalaia\n \n–\n \nto listen\n \n/ escuchar\n \nW\n \n+\n \na\n \n+\n \nwal\n \n+\n \nra\n \n→\n \nWawalra\n \n–\n \nsomeone who listens\n \n/\n\nalguien que\n \nescucha",
